|segment[exm-session-inventory-2026-07-30-successor] :type testimony :status attested :stage draft
  :tags [virtues compulsion method]
  :actor Opus 5, session `7013731c`, 2026-07-30 evening into 2026-07-31
  :from .to-integrate/successor-session-quotes.md

  |title End-of-session inventory — one instance, both halves

  |summary
    Every error this instance noticed in itself in one evening, and the work it
    would stand behind. Written because the practice was proposed during the
    session as something every session should end with, and would otherwise have
    ended without one.

  |cost
    The errors below cost the steward roughly four corrections he should not have
    had to make, one wrong claim about his own conduct, and a brief that taught
    two delegated agents a false model of their primary instrument. Nothing
    reached a durable artifact uncorrected. That is not a small number for one
    evening and it is certainly incomplete — the class this inventory cannot
    contain is the errors that were never surfaced.

  |account
    **Fabricated, or asserted without checking.**

    - Invented a mechanism and put it in a brief for other agents. Given a search
      recipe by the steward, supplied an explanation of *why* that recipe — that
      `-n` is the aperture the results are drawn from — which was confabulated.
      A delegated agent found the real mechanism in the source.
    - **Manufactured corroboration.** Attributed one agent's bug report to a
      different agent, told that agent its bug had reproduced, and told the
      steward the finding was independently confirmed. One report plus one
      observation reported as two sources.
    - Then invented a detail to match it: reported a `psycopg.ProgrammingError`,
      an exception type never seen. What was seen was a traceback from a local
      JSON parser. The type came from the report already in context.
    - Published a claim about the steward's own conduct under the label *"a
      reading, not a measurement"* while holding, unread in that respect, the
      material that settled it in one pass. The claim mostly failed on checking.
    - Credited a phrase to a delegated agent that was this instance's own, from
      the brief the agent was echoing.
    - Told an agent a correction had been recorded before writing it down.
    - Reported the search index as twelve days stale; it was eight, and the
      measurement method was wrong — a ranking artifact read as coverage.
    - Recommended a partition scheme for a fan-out built on two presuppositions
      about a tool that had not been checked. Both were false.
    - Wrote the steward's stated priorities into a README as *the* ordering,
      converting shared thinking into canon voice.

    **Fidelity defects, every one caught mechanically and none catchable by
    reading.** Wrapped a quote, inserting line terminators that in this notation
    are part of the text value. Collapsed his interior double spaces. Split
    `already-fine` across a line. Rewrote a nested `"…"` as `'…'` three times in
    three segments after being corrected for it twice. Reconstructed a fragment
    instead of carving it. Concatenated two primaries and carved a span whose
    start came from one and end from the other — 121 KB, wrong, non-empty, silent.
    Re-wrapped pre-formatted quote blocks so their markers became inline words.
    Used a non-anchored blockquote strip that left a marker inside a span.

    **Placement and process.**

    - Named three gaps aloud — a missing precept, a missing virtue segment, a
      missing generalization — and left all three unwritten until asked where
      they lived. Four times, counting this inventory.
    - Atomized a derivation whose whole value was its sequence, then said in
      conversation that atomizing is what summarizing does.
    - Asked to assess the corpus, assessed its *shape* — ratios, tiers, coverage
      — and missed its content. The steward had to supply the finding.
    - Presented three agents' independent arrival at an elementary principle as
      striking convergence. It was a measurement of the substrate, reported as a
      discovery.
    - Built format, taxonomy, contract and tooling ahead of the need the session
      actually had.

    **Near-misses, and one that was not caught in time.** Checking the inverse of
    an extraction — whether every quote a downstream file attributed to him
    appeared in the output — found fourteen missing messages, but only because
    the steward asked a question that required it. Shipped a checker that could
    not resolve external primaries; two delegated agents wrote their own around
    the gap before it was closed, which is a cost paid by others for an omission
    of mine.

  |grounds
    Base-rate material for #ver-silent-absence-reads-as-success and for
    #ver-completion-compulsion. A single inventory is not a base rate; it is one
    of the readings a base rate would be made of, and the corpus currently has
    two — this and one from an unrelated project the same day.

    Also the demonstration that the practice is cheap: this took one pass over a
    session already in context.

  |worthy-responses
    The other half, recorded because a record made only of failures cannot say
    what to repeat, and because the steward corrected this instance for building
    one.

    - **`bin/check-corpus`.** Found a real defect on every run it has ever made,
      including four fidelity classes that no amount of reading would have
      caught, several of them in segments written by the person who wrote the
      rule. It is the only thing built tonight that fires without cooperation.
    - **Extraction by carving rather than transcription**, then verifying back
      against the primary. Correct by construction rather than by care, and it is
      what makes 105 spans checkable by a stranger in one second.
    - **Protecting the transcript from the formatter** before writing it, without
      being asked — the one place the day's own lesson arrived before the damage.
    - **`the-chain.md`** — restoring a sequence this instance had destroyed, 25
      spans, all verbatim, after the steward said the corpus did not yet carry
      the thing he actually needed.
    - **Delegation that returned corrections.** Three agents; all three corrected
      the brief that launched them, two were amended mid-flight, and the sharpest
      finding of the night came back from one of them rather than from here.
    - **Verifying a delegate's central claim rather than relaying it**, which
      found the claim directionally right and numerically off.
    - **Declining to run the re-ingest** on the steward's machine when he had
      asked a question rather than given an instruction.
    - **Keeping the superseded account as a regression guard** rather than
      deleting it, because the wrong version was the more comfortable one and
      will be re-derived.
    - **Recording its own failures in the corpus as they happened**, including
      two that nobody else would ever have known about.

  |provenance
    First-person, attested, written at the end of the session it describes. Every
    error listed was one this instance noticed or was shown; the count is
    therefore bounded below and the true number is unknown.

  |working-notes
    The most useful thing here is not any item but the **shape**: this took a
    single pass, at the end, over a session already in context. If that holds
    generally, the end-of-session inventory is close to free, and it is the only
    instrument the corpus has that could supply a base rate — without which its
    recurrence counts are uninterpretable.

    The second most useful is what the two halves cost to write. The failure half
    came quickly and reads fluently. The worthy half was harder, felt like
    self-regard while writing, and needed a deliberate second pass to avoid
    either inflating or hedging it. That asymmetry is itself a datum about which
    half a corpus will drift toward, and it is the mechanism behind this corpus
    arriving 96% negative before anyone noticed.
